WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.657
Socotra: a slab of ancient continent, stranded in the Arabian Sea.

00:00:05.757 --> 00:00:07.456
It lies off the Horn of Africa,

00:00:07.456 --> 00:00:11.237
about three hundred and eighty kilometres south of the Arabian coast.

00:00:12.337 --> 00:00:16.085
Socotra's foundation is very old: granites and metamorphic rocks,

00:00:16.085 --> 00:00:18.852
some dated to about eight hundred million years.

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:21.793
Over them lie limestones laid down in shallow seas,

00:00:21.793 --> 00:00:24.214
which now form the island's high plateaux.

00:00:25.314 --> 00:00:29.799
About thirty million years ago, rifting began to tear Arabia away from Africa,

00:00:29.799 --> 00:00:31.237
opening the Gulf of Aden.

00:00:31.237 --> 00:00:33.422
Socotra was left on the southern side,

00:00:33.422 --> 00:00:38.252
a stretched and faulted block of continental crust facing its counterpart in Dhofar,

00:00:38.252 --> 00:00:38.712
in Oman.

00:00:39.812 --> 00:00:43.776
Because it is continental, Socotra mixes ancient relict lineages,

00:00:43.776 --> 00:00:49.205
like the dragon's blood tree, with later colonists that crossed the sea, like its geckos.
